    8543	### §C1 — Odlyzko zeros at height 10²² (CLOSED 2026-04-26, S71, mode I)
    8544	
    8545	**Outcome:** structural negative result + new quantitative obstruction.
    8546	
    8547	Re-ran the S49 BK arithmetic-correction probe on Odlyzko's published
    8548	tables `zeros4` (n ∈ [10²¹+1, 10²¹+10⁴], height T ≈ 1.4·10²⁰, L = 44.6)
    8549	and `zeros5` (n ∈ [10²²+1, 10²²+10⁴], T ≈ 1.4·10²¹, L = 46.8). Used a
    8550	**random-prime null** (template with primes replaced by uniform
    8551	pseudo-primes in [2, 50]) as the proper test instead of the biased
    8552	gap-shuffled null. Empirical Pearson with the BK template is
    8553	statistically indistinguishable from the random-prime null
    8554	(z = -0.94σ, +0.93σ); prime-frequency Fourier amplitudes are NOT
    8555	enhanced over random frequencies (ratios 0.95, 0.99).
    8556	
    8557	Why §C1 fails by structure: the BK signal scales as `13.6/L²` while
    8558	empirical noise scales as `4/√N`. Detection requires
    8559	**N ≥ 0.09 κ² L⁴** for κ-σ Pearson detection. At Odlyzko's heights
    8560	this needs N ≈ 3·10⁵-4·10⁵ zeros; Odlyzko publishes 10⁴, short by
    8561	~35×. Pushing to higher heights makes the situation *worse* because
    8562	L⁴ grows faster than any reasonable N. The asymptotic regime
    8563	*suppresses* the BK signal faster than data accumulation can
    8564	compensate.
    8565	
    8566	This sharpens E7.1 / E1.10 / E3.13 from qualitative "BK undetected"
    8567	to quantitative "BK detection requires N ≥ 0.81 L⁴ zeros" — a hard
    8568	scaling barrier independent of computational budget.
    8569	
    8570	See `experiments/analytic/zeta_structure/odlyzko_high_height/`
    8571	and `archive/sessions/session71_c1_odlyzko_bk_probe.md`.
    8572	
