mality extraction by CTQW on these graphs). **Distinct from**: CLOSED 105 (constructive transfer matrix), 182 (FRACTRAN), 474 (Goldreich-Levin Hadamard), and from E7.13 (Szegedy on same graphs, different mechanism). **Connection to E7.16 / E2.22**: time-averaged amplitude residual vs Cramér+odd-parity matched control is +4σ, same magnitude as E2.22's PR-resonance Cramér closure — primes are density-and-parity Cramér-typical at the time-averaged CTQW amplitude level. **Cross-domain ingredient (Childs continuous-time quantum walks) performed real work**: first project use of CTQW spectral-density framework (D4 Szegedy uses discriminant-matrix algebra, fundamentally different); CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §1 row "Continuous-time quantum walks (CTQW)" promoted PROPOSED (D5) → USED E with edge E7.20. **Channelled mathematician**: Childs (CTQW spectral-density framework). **Successors proposed (S141)**: D5.a CTQW on LPS Ramanujan Cayley graph `Cay(PSL_2(F_p), prime-indexed quaternion generators)` — non-abelian Cayley with explicit Ramanujan spectral gap; tests whether non-abelian Cayley admits isolated band-edge clusters that abelian divisor / coprime do not (composes with D28 PROPOSED, 1-2 sessions); D5.b multi-vertex seed `|v_s⟩ = (1/√k) Σ_{i ∈ S} |i⟩` for arithmetic-relevant S (powers of 2, smooth numbers, square-free integers) — does the right seed unlock polylog amplitude on primes? (1-session screening). Cites E7.13 (D4 Szegedy structural template), E7.16 (Friedman density+parity matching), E2.22 (PR resonance Cramér closure structurally analogous). Cross-domain refs: Childs 2009 *PRL* 102, 180501 = arXiv:0806.1972; Childs-Cleve-Deotto-Farhi-Gutmann-Spielman 2003 STOC = arXiv:quant-ph/0209131. See `experiments/quantum/ctqw_chi_p/` and `archive/sessions/session141_d5_ctqw_chi_p.md`. | 141 |
    | §G2 ATTACK_VECTORS — Liouville and Möbius Gowers `U^k` norms: empirical decay-rate test of Green-Tao Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality theorem at scale (cross-domain: Green-Tao 2012 *Annals* 175 = arXiv:0807.1736 + Green-Tao-Ziegler 2012 *Annals* 176 = arXiv:1009.3998 inverse U^{s+1} theorem) | FAIL | E | Frontier wild_swing S153 on the additive-combinatorics analogue of S100 (G1, λ Anderson Lyapunov closed E). Linear-sieve Liouville `λ(n) = (-1)^Ω(n) ∈ {-1,+1}` and Möbius `μ(n) = λ(n)·1[n squarefree] ∈ {-1,0,+1}` for n ∈ [0, N). Centred Gowers `U^2` via FFT identity `||f||_{U^2}^4 = (1/N^4) Σ_k |fhat(k)|^4`; centred `U^3` via Cauchy-Schwarz recursion `||f||_{U^3}^8 = (1/N) Σ_h ||Δ_h f||_{U^2}^4` with `Δ_h f(x) = f(x) f(x+h)`. **Pre-stated falsifiers**: F-A any sustained \|Z\| > 5σ deviation at any (N, energy) NOT W-tricked away (A-grade, would falsify GT empirically); F-E λ U^k matches IID Rademacher within sample noise across N ≤ 2^20 ⇒ B-grade closure mode E; F-I empirical decay rate matches Korevaar 2002-style bound up to constants. **Outcome: F-E holds globally, A-grade prevented; B-grade negative.** **λ U^2 (single realisation vs 30–50 IID Rademacher seeds at each N ∈ {2^10, 2^12, 2^14, 2^16, 2^18, 2^20})**: ratio λ_u2_pow4 / Rademacher_mean = 0.940, 0.932, 0.981, 0.992, 0.993, 0.999 — converges to 1 monotonically with finite-N deficit `1 - O(1/√N)`. Z-scores: -2.21, -3.07, -1.88, -1.39, -2.36, -0.74 σ — all within ±3.5σ noise floor (Bonferroni for 6 N-tests = 2.64σ; only N=4096 exceeds, sub-Bonferroni when accounting for multiple comparisons). OLS fit `log(||λ||_{U^2}^4) = -0.990·log(N) + 0.564` matches Rademacher prediction `-1.000·log(N) + log(2) = -1.000·log(N) + 0.693` within slope error 1%; intercept gap consistent with finite-N centring noise. **μ U^2 (N up to 2^20)**: matched-variance IID prediction `2(6/π²)²/N = 0.7392/N` matched to 0.998 ratio at N=2^20 (1.293 → 0.998 across N range). The huge nominal Z = -433σ vs ±1 Rademacher reflects only the σ² = 6/π² < 1 variance gap; against matched-variance IID the convergence to 1 is exact. **λ U^3 (full-h, N ∈ {1024, 2048, 4096, 8192})**: ratio to Rademacher pool mean = 1.00, 1.05, 0.75, 0.61 — Z-scores +0.87, +1.44, +0.66, +1.88 σ, all within 2σ noise. **Inverse-theorem stress (1-step nilsequence detector)**: max single Fourier coefficient `max_k |λhat(k)|²` matches Rademacher max-coefficient distribution (CLT prediction `√(N log N)`) within \|Z\| ≤ 3.01σ at every tested N up to 2^20. The single +3.01σ at N=2^18 returns to noise (-0.01σ) at N=2^20 — no sustained 1-step nilsequence correlation. μ small-denominator clustering at N ≤ 2^14 is FFT-bin alignment artefact (bins better resolve rationals 1/30, 1/6 at small N; disperses at large N where FFT-bin spacing 1/N misaligns with rationals of denominator 30). **Mechanism for closure mode E**: GT's Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality theorem `Σ_n μ(n) F(g(n)Γ) = O_F(N log^{-A} N)` for any nilsequence `F(g(n)Γ)` predicts (via GT-Ziegler U^{s+1}[N] inverse theorem) `||μ||_{U^k} = o(1)` for every k. **Empirically the bound is much sharper at finite N**: not just `o(1)` but matching IID matched-variance prediction within 1% at N = 2^20. The GT log-power refinement `log^{-A} N` is below the empirical noise floor `1/√N · 1/√(n_seeds)` at every tested N. **Pairs with E2.18 (S100, λ Anderson Lyapunov) to give two orthogonal multiplicative-regime confirmations**: spectral (E2.18) and additive-combinatorics (E2.25) categories both confirm λ ≈ Rademacher without W-tricking. **Stark contrast with E2.13 (chi_P Gowers, S85)**: chi_P's U^k norms detected the Hardy-Littlewood singular series mod q (88σ at parity resonance, removable by W=2310 sieve to ~4σ); λ and μ have NO mod-q signal, no W-trick needed. **Adds EDGE E2.25**: `||λ||_{U^k[Z/NZ]}^{2^k} = ||IID(σ_λ=1)||_{U^k}^{2^k} · (1 ± O(1/√N))` for k ∈ {2, 3} at N ≤ 2^20; same for μ with σ_μ² = 6/π². EVS shape (forbids 1-step or 2-step nilsequence correlation visible at finite N). **Adds 3 new pseudorandomness measures** (λ U^2, μ U^2, λ U^3) to the project's existing 38-measure battery — first measurements on multiplicative functions in the Gowers-norm family. **Cross-domain ingredient (Green-Tao Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality theorem) performed real work**: first project use; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §3 row "Gowers norms / Green-Tao Möbius orthogonality" promoted PROPOSED → USED-E with edges E2.13 (S85, χ_P) and E2.25 (this session, λ + μ). **Channelled mathematician**: Tao (additive combinatorics; Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality). **Successors proposed (S153)**: G2.a von Mangoldt `Λ(n)` Gowers norms — bridges additive (χ_P) and multiplicative (λ, μ) regimes; log-weighted variance σ_Λ² ≈ N/log N varies with N (1 session); G2.b higher-order Gowers `U^4`, `U^5` of λ — tests whether λ has 3-step or 4-step nilsequence correlations at finite N (subsample h_1, ..., h_{k-1} for tractability; 1-2 sessions); G2.c rescheduled §G3 (Möbius Voronin) — inverse-theorem absence raises priority of effective universality of `1/ζ` (2 sessions). Cites E2.13, E2.14, E2.18, E1.10, E3.13. Cross-domain refs: Green-Tao 2012 arXiv:0807.1736; Green-Tao-Ziegler 2012 arXiv:1009.3998; Green-Tao 2010 arXiv:math/0606088; Sarnak 2010 IAS lectures; Tao 2016 arXiv:1509.05422 (logarithmic-Chowla). See `experiments/information_theory/liouville_gowers_uk/`, `archive/sessions/session153_g2_liouville_mobius_gowers_uk.md`. | 153 |
    | C3.a (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES §1, S105 successor) — Arithmetic-primitive bounded-Kt VM × per-bit difficulty: does adding LOG2 / LI_APPROX / DIV_LOG / GEO_SUM to the bounded-Kt simulator shift the cut on `s_J^(N) := (bit_J(π(0)),...,bit_J(π(2^N-1)))` from S105's `J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N` toward E1.3's `⌈N/2⌉`? | REFINEMENT | E | Composition of E5.8 (Brandt-class barrier — structural argument inherited unchanged) + E1.3 (per-bit difficulty profile — refined inline). Built 4-bit-per-op extended VM (16 ops: 8 base {PUSH0/1/N, INC, ADD, SUB, MUL, SHR1, DUP, EMIT_S, EMIT0, HALT} + 4 R^{-1}-kernel arithmetic primitives {LOG2, LI_APPROX, DIV_LOG, GEO_SUM}) with integer stack alongside bit-emit stream; T_MAX = 4096, INT_CAP = 10⁹. C inner loop (`sim.c → sim.so`) batch-evaluates one program against all targets simultaneously; pre-skip of programs with no EMIT in pc cycle saves ~50% of enumeration. **Pre-stated falsifiers**: F1 (full shift to ⌈N/2⌉ — every easy-J compresses, every hard-J saturates); F2 (no shift — easy zone all saturated as in original VM, DUPLICATE-PLUS); F3 (intermediate hierarchy — some easy J compress, others saturate). **Result: F3 holds globally (intermediate hierarchy)**, with structure: per-N at L_max=24 (matched to target_lens 16/32/64), the cut moves fully to ⌈N/2⌉ for N ∈ {4, 5} and reverts to J*(N) for N=6; at L_max=28, N=6 partially shifts (J=4 compresses, J=3 still saturates). **Within-easy-zone J-monotone hierarchy identified**: bits closer to J*(N) (the trivial-zero boundary) compress at smaller L_max; bits closer to ⌈N/2⌉ (the E1.3 hard-easy boundary) require larger L_max — the bounded-Kt cost of bit-J of π is empirically monotone in N − J. **Headline compressing programs (L_max=24)**: (N=4, J=2) Kt_b'=33 prog `ADD, LI, LI, EMIT_S, PUSH_N, PUSH_N` — emits `bit_0(LI(LI(2x)))` which EXACTLY matches `bit_2(π(x))` on [0, 16); (N=5, J=3) Kt_b'=33 prog `EMIT_S, LI, DUP, INC, PUSH_N, PUSH0`. Triple-LI program `EMIT_S, PUSH_N, LI, LI, LI, DUP, EMIT_S` (L=28) compresses (N=6, J=4). **Falsified**: original C3 spec's S105 prediction "if YES, R^{-1}-kernel-equipped VM resolves the smooth/oscillatory transition" was correct AT SMALL N (4, 5) but breaks at N=6; the predicted full shift is L_max-budget limited. **Negative content**: hard-zone (J < ⌈N/2⌉) cells remain saturated at L_max ≤ target_len for every tested N ≥ 4; no compressing programs found within budget for hard bits. The structural orthogonality of Brandt's TRAVERSE to the per-bit family (all four obstructions O1-O4 of E5.8) is unaffected by VM choice — empirical compressibility of some `s_J^(N)` does not enable a Brandt diagonalisation. **Quantitative regime caveat**: at L_max > target_len the hard-easy distinction begins to fray (e.g. L_max=28 spuriously "compresses" N=4 hard zone J=0,1 because 28-bit programs can express any 16-bit pattern combinatorially); the meaningful regime is L_max ≤ target_len. **Refines E1.3 inline** with VM-richness hierarchy: 3-bit stack VM cuts at J*(N), 4-bit arith VM cuts at ⌈N/2⌉ for N ≤ 5 with within-easy-zone monotone J-ranking. Cross-domain ingredient: arithmetic-augmented bounded-Kt simulator (analogous to Levin Kt with non-trivial primitive set) — first project use of Li-kernel primitives inside Kt simulator. **Adds new finite-N empirical fact** (not previously measured): bounded-Kt cut location is VM-richness-AND-N-dependent with explicit hierarchy. Cites E5.8 (unchanged), E1.3 (refined inline), E1.5, E1.9 (LSB still saturates), E5.3. Successors proposed (S150): C3.a.i L_max=32 sweep (resolves N=6, J=3); C3.a.ii larger N at random-program sampling; C3.a.iii VM-budget vs RH-scale linkage to S146 F1 results; C3.a.iv ablation drop/swap of arithmetic primitives. See `experiments/constructions/brandt_per_bit_arith_vm/`, `archive/sessions/session150_c3a_arith_vm.md`. | 150 |
    | C3.a.iv (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES §1, S150 successor) — Arithmetic-primitive ABLATION of the C3.a bounded-Kt VM: are any of {LOG2, LI, DIV_LOG, GEO_SUM} strictly necessary for the easy-zone cut shift documented in S150? | REFINEMENT | E | Composition of E5.8 (unchanged) + E1.3 (refined inline) + C3.a/S150 (refined). Re-ran S150's per-bit Kt_b'(s_J^(N)) scan over N ∈ {3,4,5,6}, J = 0..N−1 at L_max=28 under SIX ablation conditions: baseline, drop_LOG2, drop_LI, drop_DIV_LOG, drop_GEO_SUM, only_LI (drop other three). C inner loop (`sim_ablation.so`) extends `batch_enumerate_at_length_filtered` with a 16-bit `forbidden_ops_mask`; programs containing any forbidden op are skipped pre-simulation (exact ablation, no noise). Total 6 × 268M = 1.6B programs in 463s wall-time. **Pre-stated falsifiers (5 outcomes)**: F1 (LI solely necessary AND sufficient for easy zone); F2 (multiple primitives jointly necessary); F3 (LI alone insufficient); F4 (no single primitive strictly necessary); F5 mixed/incoherent. **Result: F4 holds for the easy zone** {(3,2), (4,2), (5,3), (6,4)} — every cell that compresses in baseline also compresses under every single-drop AND under only_LI. drop_LI matches baseline at L_max≤24 cells with alternative compressing programs: (N=3,J=2) `EMIT_S, PUSH_N, GEO_SUM, EMIT_S`; (N=4,J=2) `GEO_SUM, DIV_LOG, EMIT_S, PUSH_N, INC, PUSH_N`; (N=5,J=3) `EMIT_S, INC, LOG2, DUP, PUSH_N, PUSH0` (LOG2 alone); and is +1 bit at (N=6,J=4) (`EMIT_S, GEO_SUM, PUSH_N, ADD, DUP, LOG2, PUSH0` Kt 37 vs baseline LI-triple Kt 36). **Refutes S150's narrowest reading** that "iterated LI is the dominant compression mechanism" — LI is the cleanest realization but is one of four substitutable mechanisms. only_LI matches baseline on every easy-zone cell, confirming LI is sufficient; drop_LI also preserves compression, confirming LI is not necessary. The arithmetic-primitive class {LOG2, LI, DIV_LOG, GEO_SUM} forms an OVER-COMPLETE basis for E1.3-easy-zone bounded-Kt compression at the tested (N, L_max). **Hard-zone primitive sensitivity differs**: meaningful (L<target_len) cell (N=5, J=2) requires LI ∧ DIV_LOG (drop_LI saturates, drop_DIV_LOG saturates, drop_LOG2 and drop_GEO_SUM preserve) — the compressing program `DIV_LOG, LI, SHR1, SHR1, EMIT_S, PUSH_N, PUSH0` realizes a structurally distinct floor(out_count/log(LI(out_count)))>>2 mod 2 mechanism. The other "hard-zone" cells with apparent ablation-sensitivity (N=4, J=0,1) live in the L_max>target_len combinatorial regime (E1.3 caveat (a)) and are not load-bearing. **Closure mode E** (refinement of E1.3, no asymptotic improvement). **Refines E1.3 inline** with primitive-class-robustness statement: the S150 cut shift is driven by the FAMILY of slow-growing-integer-function primitives, not by LI specifically. **Refines C3.a/S150** by demonstrating that the optimum-program disassembly does not faithfully indicate which primitives are causally required. Cites E5.8 (unchanged), E1.3 (refined), E1.5, E5.3. Successors proposed (S158): C3.a.iv.α LI-vs-non-LI gap scaling at large N (test whether LI gains strict advantage at N≥7); C3.a.iv.β alternative primitive set {LN, INV_LI, ZETA_K} (test class-robustness across primitive sets). See `experiments/constructions/brandt_per_bit_arith_vm_ablation/`, `archive/sessions/session158_c3aiv_arith_vm_ablation.md`. | 158 |
